  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpper level disturbance will be sliding by to the north today\nacross southern Canada. The trough axis looks to be crossing our\narea this morning and will be shifting towards northern New\nEngland for this afternoon during peak heating. Even though the\ntrough axis will be to our east, some isolated to scattered\nthunderstorms will still be possible over our northern areas for\nthis afternoon thanks to the strong jet overhead. There will be\nplenty of shear in place today (0-6 km bulk shear values above\n40 kts according to the 00z HRRR), but overall instability will\nbe on the lower side (generally around 1000 J/kg or less). Lapse\nrates aloft look fairly weak and there will be plenty of dry air\naloft, which may limit the overall coverage of storms. While a\nrogue stronger storm cannot be totally ruled out, the coverage\ntoday looks fairly limited. CAMs suggest the threat for any\nstorms will be just across the Adirondacks, Lake George-Saratoga\nRegion and southern Vermont. SPC has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of\n5) across northern New England, which makes sense based on the\noverall timing of the trough axis and where the better forcing\nwill focus the higher coverage of storms. 00z SPC HREF also\nshows the better updraft helicity will remain northeast of the\nregion for today as well. With the limited coverage of\nthunderstorms, the threat for heavy rainfall and flash flooding\nis more limited as well and PWATs look much lower than recent\ndays as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBehind another disturbance aloft, upper level trough will be \nlocated over the Northeast for late Friday into Saturday, with\nlower heights and cooler temps aloft. This will allow for much\ncooler temps across the region, beginning on Friday night, when\ngood radiational cooling will lead to widespread lows in the\n50s, with even some 40s across the high terrain. Despite\nabundant sunshine, the cool temps aloft will only allow for\nhighs in the 70s on Saturday. Albany Airport has reached into\nthe 80s each day this month so far, so this will be noticeable.\nHumidity levels look more comfortable as well, with dewpoints\nonly in the 50s to near 60.  \n\nThe cooler temperatures are expected to continue into Sunday as\nwell, although the threat for rain looks to return. Models are\nin good agreement now on the approach of an upper level\ndisturbance within the westerly flow aloft. Along with the upper\nlevel disturbance, a surface wave of low pressure looks to move\nright across the region, with a widespread cluster of showers\nand thunderstorms for Sunday into Sunday night. NBM POPs are now\nin the likely range for the entire area for Sunday afternoon-\nevening and this makes sense based on the latest guidance. Will\nneed to watch for the potential for some embedded heavier bursts\nof rainfall, although the system should be fairly progressive\noverall. NBM already shows a 20% to 40% chance for 1\" or rain \nor greater, so will continue to monitor model QPF trends.",
